      "heading": "What does a **machine** know about depth?",
      "sentence": "Lecture C of three. Every camera, from a 1646 dark room to a 3-D program, **projects** a scene onto a plane — but only some of them ==know the scene==.",
      "prompt": "Master Lecture cycle · Volume perception and depth · Digital Creativity I",
      "image_brief": "Kircher's 1646 portable camera obscura: a person inside a box traces the projected, inverted world.",
      "notes": "- Lecture A: the viewer builds volume. B: the rule. C: the machine.\n- Course rule: in class we use local tools only.",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-VD-C.cover"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "analysis-opener",
      "slide_role": "analysis_opener",
      "background_kind": "geometrical",
      "heading": "Open",
      "sentence": "",
      "notes": "- Eight-step card, steps 1–3; add one question: which engine made this?"
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "analysis-model",
      "slide_role": "analysis_model",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Cubic Structure.jpg",
      "heading": "Every cue, and **no cloth**",
      "body": [
        {
          "kicker": "Describe",
          "text": "A white open cube stands on a checkered floor. List the cues: **cast shadow**, **shading**, the **shrinking** squares."
        },
        {
          "kicker": "Engine",
          "text": "Photograph, 3-D render or AI image? Say what in the picture **decides** — and what would not."
        },
        {
          "kicker": "Critical",
          "text": "It is a 3-D render. Perfect cues prove the **software** works. What would they prove about a garment ==made of real cloth==?"
        }
      ],
      "image_brief": "A computer-rendered hollow cube on a checkered floor with soft shadows.",
      "notes": "- Keep the cue ledger from lecture A at hand.",
      "media_slot_id": "ML-VD-C.analysis-model"
